# Everything the build does not need, plus everything it must never see.
#
# The two entries that matter most are node_modules and .env: the first because a
# macOS install would overwrite the Linux one the image just made (wrong Prisma
# engine, wrong Copilot CLI binary), the second because a build context is not a
# place for secrets — anyone who can pull the image can read them.

# Dependencies - reinstalled inside the image for the target platform.
node_modules
.pnp
.pnp.js

# Build output - produced inside the image.
.next
.next-*
out
dist
build
*.tsbuildinfo

# Caches
.turbo
coverage

# Secrets and local config.
#
# The ** prefixes are not stylistic, even now that configuration lives in a single
# root .env. A bare `.env` matches the repo root and nothing else, which once let
# a per-app env file - GitHub client secret, session secret and all - into the
# build context. Keeping the recursive form means a stray copy anywhere in the
# tree cannot leak in either. Nothing in the build reads these files: the
# .env.example is documentation and the real values arrive as runtime
# environment variables.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/.mcp.json

# Version control and CI
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Editors and OS noise
.vscode
.idea
.kiro
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
*.pem

# Docs and local tooling that no build step reads
docs
README.md
LICENSE
todo.md
*.log
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
